diff --git a/docs/worker-first-v1.md b/docs/worker-first-v1.md
index ce3d31404d..9d077ecfd2 100644
--- a/docs/worker-first-v1.md
+++ b/docs/worker-first-v1.md
@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@
 Workers always win. The Board reserves the origin hub for a portable card only when **every** compatible member of the origin's *configured* pool (never the request subset) is known, fresh and refused solely for pressure (score >= .85) or full slots, AND the hub's reported measured CPU busy is fresh (conservative end within 15 s) and strictly below the operator's threshold, AND hub capacity/memory/drain allow it, AND no other hub overflow reservation is outstanding, AND the sample's conservative start is after the last hub overflow grant. Unknown, stale, held, draining or offline workers veto overflow and the card queues. Zero compatible workers queues with `no_eligible_worker` (overflow path only; with overflow off the same card queues with the per-candidate rollup `capability_mismatch` or `mixed`, and core treats that as the same state when every configured pool member carries a structural `supports()` reason): the automatic structural origin repin was removed (only the explicit owner repin clears the fence). The origin then posts one owner notice per card revision (`hermes_cli/kanban_repin_notice.py`): a comment naming the missing labels and the exact `hermes kanban move <id> --runs-on machine:<origin> --reason "no pool worker can run it"` command, plus a `worker_repin_notice` event its origin chat receives; the dispatcher stuck summary lists such cards as "waiting for a manual repin" instead of a stall. Nothing repins automatically. Prior runs, host-bound cards and running leases are never moved.
 
 * Board: operator-owned `worker_overflow` setting, e.g. `{"hub": {"machine": "hub", "cpu_busy_below_pct": 50}}`; `{}`/absent disables it. The lease grant re-runs the decision with the current registry and records the consumed sample; a worker freeing up, a CPU rise, a used sample or a disabled setting refuses the grant.
-* Core: `kanban.admission.cpu_busy_below_pct` in the **installation-root** config enables (a) a 10 s metrics report carrying `cpu_busy_pct`/`cpu_busy_window_ms`/`cpu_busy_sample_end_ms` (2 s hard total, never replayed), (b) a lock-free advisory pre-check before any Board lease (sampler sample at most 10 s old), and (c) the final gate: with `admission.lock` held and before the claim commits, the same advisory sampler sample (at most 15 s old, re-read from `cache/hub-cpu-sample.json`, never a new sample under the lock) must be strictly below the limit and the host running count below the root `kanban.max_in_progress`, inside the 2.25 s hold. Any unknown refuses, each with its own reason in the skip event and queue line: an unreadable config or bad value is `hub_cpu_limit_unknown`, a deadline already passed is `hub_cpu_gate_expired` (or `hub_cpu_gate_expired_counting` when it passed during the census), a missing/stale/malformed sample is `hub_cpu_sample_stale`, and a hold overrun is `hub_admission_overrun`. The lock-free advisory alone reports `hub_cpu_unknown`. The 5 s gap between the two ages keeps a sample the advisory accepted fresh through the lease round-trip, so the hub does not take a lease it then cannot use. (Until 2026-11-13 the gate took an independent ~1 s sample under the lock and counted the host twice around it; in the GIL-busy hub gateway that overran the hold after every lease and was reported as `hub_cpu_unknown` while the advisory was fresh.) The gate applies to every new ready/review claim on that host (legacy, host-bound and policy cards); the worker load formula is not used for the origin's own cards.
+* Core: `kanban.admission.cpu_busy_below_pct` in the **installation-root** config enables (a) a 10 s metrics report carrying `cpu_busy_pct`/`cpu_busy_window_ms`/`cpu_busy_sample_end_ms` (2 s hard total, never replayed), (b) a lock-free advisory pre-check before any Board lease (sampler sample at most 10 s old), and (c) the final gate: with `admission.lock` held and before the claim commits, the same advisory sampler sample (at most 15 s old, re-read from `cache/hub-cpu-sample.json`, never a new sample under the lock) must be strictly below the limit and the host running count below the root `kanban.max_in_progress`, inside the 2.25 s hold. Any unknown refuses, each with its own reason in the skip event and queue line: an unreadable config or bad value is `hub_cpu_limit_unknown`, a deadline already passed is `hub_cpu_gate_expired` (or `hub_cpu_gate_expired_counting` when it passed during the census), a missing/stale/malformed sample is `hub_cpu_sample_stale`, and a hold overrun is `hub_admission_overrun`. The lock-free advisory alone reports `hub_cpu_unknown` (its sample missing, stale, future-dated or malformed), and `hub_cpu_pool_limit_unknown` when it cannot read the priority-pool limit it compares against. The 5 s gap between the two ages keeps a sample the advisory accepted fresh through the lease round-trip, so the hub does not take a lease it then cannot use. (Until 2026-11-13 the gate took an independent ~1 s sample under the lock and counted the host twice around it; in the GIL-busy hub gateway that overran the hold after every lease and was reported as `hub_cpu_unknown` while the advisory was fresh.) The gate applies to every new ready/review claim on that host (legacy, host-bound and policy cards); the worker load formula is not used for the origin's own cards.
 * Rollout order: deploy Boards and clients first; set root `cpu_busy_below_pct`; verify reports; only then enable Board `worker_overflow`. Rollback: set Board `worker_overflow` to `{}` and read it back (outstanding hub reservations become unclaimable). Rollback keeps the root CPU guard and `kanban.max_in_progress` (7): removing the root key or raising the cap is a separate, explicit operator decision, never part of rollback. After rollback the Board still admits the origin hub's review continuation of a card it already ran on the hub (review ruling 11), on the hub's non-CPU admission only (active, unheld, pickup, capability, fresh metrics, no drain, memory < 85 %, a free slot under a known cap); the root CPU guard is then that claim's only CPU check, which is why rollback keeps it. Rollback never creates an overflow, a hub reservation or a new hub assignment, and there is no operator move of a review card to ready: a review card the hub can never admit moves only once its refusal reason is cleared or overflow is re-enabled.
 * The advisory is fed only by the sync loop's advisory sampler thread (`hermes_cli/machine_cpu_sampler.py`, `cache/hub-cpu-sample.json`): a full ~1 s sample every 5 s on its own thread, written independently of the Board POST, so neither a Board outage nor a slow sync round (3-20 s on the hub) starves it. Each metrics report attaches the newest sample not yet reported (at most 10 s old). The loop starts the thread and stops it when the loop ends, so when the sync loop is not running (sync role given up, no identity) every hub claim on that host defers as `hub_cpu_unknown`: fail-closed by design. When the advisory stays stale for 60 s of dispatcher observation while the root limit is on, the dispatcher opens one Kanban card per incident (`hermes_cli/kanban_hub_cpu_alert.py`, key `hub-cpu-advisory-stale`). An earlier design measured inside the loop and validated the 1-2 s window across loop iterations; on the hub every sample was dropped (2026-11-12).
 * Turns are not refused by a claim's sample: on **every** host (whether or not the root limit is set), chat, notification and review turns wait out a busy `admission.lock` for longer than its longest designed hold (`admission_fence.CLAIM_MAX_HOLD_S`), a waiting turn makes the next claims defer to their next tick (also on hosts without the CPU gate, e.g. worker-1: `BUSY_WAIT_S` rose from 0.5 s to 3.0 s for all turn sources), and `prompt.admit` waits outside the session resume lock and then retries its admission once (nothing durable precedes admission, so no duplicate turn).
